            <ArticleTitle>Gabapentin hybrid peptides and bioconjugates.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1479-86</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.bmc.2013.12.017</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0968-0896(13)01011-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Synthetic approaches to gabapentin bioconjugates that overcome the tendency of gabapentin to cyclize into its γ-lactam are studied. Gabapentin was converted by N-acylation at its N-terminus into di-, tri-, and tetrapeptides (L-Ala-Gbp, L-Val-Gbp, L-Ala-L-Phe-Gbp, Gly-L-Ala-β-Ala-Gbp). Carboxyl-activated Boc-protected gabapentin was used to N-, O-, and S-acylate small peptides and hormones to give conjugates that could also provide prodrugs containing conformationally constrained gabapentin units.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Copyright © 2014. Published by Elsevier Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
